<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TActiveClientScript</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.2" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveClientScript" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TActiveClientScript</tt> is the active control counterpart of
<a href="?page=Controls.ClientScript"><tt>TClientScript</tt></a>. It carries JavaScript
code or a JavaScript file into the page, and it works inside a callback response. The
functions and variables that the code declares stay available to the page after the
callback finishes.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The control takes the properties of <tt>TClientScript</tt>. The body of the tag holds the
code, and the <tt>ScriptUrl</tt> property names a file instead.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TActiveClientScript ID="Script1" Visible="false"&gt;
	window.refreshChart = function (data) {
		// ...
	};
&lt;/com:TActiveClientScript&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function buttonCallback($sender, $param)
{
	$this->Script1->render($param->getNewWriter());
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>How a callback delivers the script</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control renders differently in the two kinds of request.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Request</th><th>Result</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>a normal request or a postback</td>
	<td>The control writes a <tt>&lt;script&gt;</tt> element in place. The browser runs the code at that point in the document.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>a callback</td>
	<td>The control hands the code to the callback client rather than to the writer. PRADO runs the code after it applies the DOM updates of the response.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
A <tt>ScriptUrl</tt> follows the same split. A normal request renders a
<tt>&lt;script src&gt;</tt> element, and a callback registers the file with the client
script manager under the control's client ID.
</p>

<h2>Timing</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Code that arrives in a callback runs at a different moment than the same code in a normal
response. A normal response runs each block where it sits in the document, in order, while
the browser parses. A callback response runs its blocks together, after every DOM update
lands and after any dynamically included script file loads. Code that depends on running
next to the markup around it behaves differently in the two cases.
</p>

<h2>Rendering the control</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The control writes its script when it renders. A control declared with
<tt>Visible="false"</tt> skips the initial page render, and a callback handler renders it
by calling <tt>render</tt> with a new writer. A control that sits inside an active
container renders whenever that container renders.
</p>

